                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "aa254aa3-05d0-4225-be28-dc4921df2ad9",
                        "source_entry_id": "001bea41-1ca3-3786-8aca-f066a9113c76",
                        "source_sense_id": "30dccdba-67f5-30ad-a1cc-f71bf50ad30d",
                        "target_lang_word": "doubleness",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "doubleness",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "db844f25-8686-362f-87ca-a309e10e7779",
                                        "entry_id": "f4fe855d-0bca-3ce6-8000-a0dafdeeee74",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The state of being double or doubled."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "578d8edf-b46b-4cfc-a915-925e7d21674c",
                                                "sense_id": "db844f25-8686-362f-87ca-a309e10e7779",
                                                "text": "1540, Great Bible, 1 Chronicles 12 [verse 33],\nAnd of Zabulon that went out to the battayle and proceded forth to the war, with all maner of instrumentes of war fyftie M. that were prepared to the war, without any doublenesse of herte."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4ee405cb-df8c-453d-891b-34e52e02b062",
                                                "sense_id": "db844f25-8686-362f-87ca-a309e10e7779",
                                                "text": "If you think well to carry this as you may, the doubleness of the benefit defends the deceit from reproof."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fa0e3e1c-571f-4faa-936e-6de8999b0e13",
                                                "sense_id": "db844f25-8686-362f-87ca-a309e10e7779",
                                                "text": "I only know myself as a human entity; the scene, so to speak, of thoughts and affections; and am sensible of a certain doubleness by which I can stand as remote from myself as from another. However intense my experience, I am conscious of the presence and criticism of a part of me, which, as it were, is not a part of me, but spectator, sharing no experience, but taking note of it;"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "55c09468-789e-47fa-b8c4-4a85c8a652fd",
                                                "sense_id": "db844f25-8686-362f-87ca-a309e10e7779",
                                                "text": "It was easy for him, when he saw the straight march of his school, the old section taller and wider and brighter than the new wing, and the new wing following in its narrow dark doubleness, to think of boxcars."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "166d14b9-630b-3f21-b068-76566b1dfe52",
                                        "entry_id": "f4fe855d-0bca-3ce6-8000-a0dafdeeee74",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Behaviour intended to deceive people."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3a036572-a01c-42c1-a654-d42761c5d7fd",
                                                "sense_id": "166d14b9-630b-3f21-b068-76566b1dfe52",
                                                "text": "But if it be a vice to coulour craftinesse, subtile practises, doublenesse and hollow behauiour, with a cloke of pollicie, amitie and wisedome, then are Comineus and his companie to be reputed vicious."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b147cb90-5482-4b0a-835d-33d5901488a7",
                                                "sense_id": "166d14b9-630b-3f21-b068-76566b1dfe52",
                                                "text": "It is clear to you, I hope, that Stephen was not a hypocrite—capable of deliberate doubleness for a selfish end; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ee5def02-c9a7-42ce-8a99-ba81c781386e",
                                                "sense_id": "166d14b9-630b-3f21-b068-76566b1dfe52",
                                                "text": "I plummeted down [the ski hill] past the zigzaggers, the students, the experts, through year after year of doubleness and smiles and compromise, into my own past."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "410c6904-de9b-4bdb-bd71-1270e73bb75a",
                        "source_entry_id": "001bea41-1ca3-3786-8aca-f066a9113c76",
                        "source_sense_id": "30dccdba-67f5-30ad-a1cc-f71bf50ad30d",
                        "target_lang_word": "dualité",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "dualité",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "85f90cdb-3bf1-3ed2-abf0-e71b998f337f",
                                        "entry_id": "5323ba7c-ceb4-371c-8bdf-edca553bfe39",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Caractère ou état de ce qui est double."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0b44abe8-aa88-49ae-9375-abf349cd8de6",
                                                "sense_id": "85f90cdb-3bf1-3ed2-abf0-e71b998f337f",
                                                "text": "Ainsi, comme il y a deux principes, l'un mâle et l'autre femelle, l'un actif et l'autre passif, dans toute dualité, quelle qu'elle soit, l'un des termes sera mâle et actif, l'autre passif et femelle ; […]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "60f6e2e5-8726-4a8f-82d5-874817194de1",
                                                "sense_id": "85f90cdb-3bf1-3ed2-abf0-e71b998f337f",
                                                "text": "– Comment expliquez-vous, monsieur le professeur, me dit-il brusquement vers la fin du dîner, comment expliquez-vous la dualité ou la duplicité de notre nature ?…\nEt, comme il s'aperçut que je ne le comprenais pas parfaitement, il reprit :\n– Ne vous êtes-vous jamais trouvé au haut d'une tour ou bien au bord d'un précipice, ayant à la fois la tentation de vous élancer dans le vide et un sentiment de terreur absolument contraire ?…"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "411db5b3-eb89-4d6a-b8bd-7e7f8895ee6c",
                                                "sense_id": "85f90cdb-3bf1-3ed2-abf0-e71b998f337f",
                                                "text": "Je cherchai donc à me représenter la dualité onde-corpuscule par une image spatiale où le corpuscule serait le centre d'un phénomène ondulatoire étendu."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "13fd1965-955b-3e7c-9c75-ec165023da8e",
                                        "entry_id": "5323ba7c-ceb4-371c-8bdf-edca553bfe39",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Fait d'être dual pour un graphe."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Théorie des graphes"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "884ae418-732a-44e8-aa65-635aabed2c2e",
                        "source_entry_id": "001bea41-1ca3-3786-8aca-f066a9113c76",
